{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'synaptic|presynaptic|postsynaptic|input to|receives.*input|connected to|connects to|RO_00024|RO_001500|RO_001900|RO_0002120|RO_000241' src/ontology/cl-edit.owl\"","aggregated_output":"4932:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D011689\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12907269\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:14568361\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31424738\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33288911\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38168772\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000121 \"An inhibitory neuron and the sole output neuron of the cerebellar cortex, the Purkinje cell's soma is located between the granular and molecular layers of the cerebellum. It is one of the largest neural cells in the mammalian brain, ranging from 50 to 80 micrometres in diameter. Purkinje cells have planar, fan-shaped dendrites that branch extensively with little overlap. This cell type receives synaptic input from parallel fibres, which modulate high-frequency spike activity known as \\\"simple spikes,\\\" and climbing fibres, which modulate infrequent calcium spike activity known as \\\"complex spikes\\\". Purkinje cells are involved in motor coordination, particularly in correcting movements in progress.\")\n5122:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D010011\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000137 \"A mature osteoblast that has become embedded in the bone matrix. They occupy a small cavity, called lacuna, in the matrix and are connected to adjacent osteocytes via protoplasmic projections called canaliculi.\")\n8569:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000561 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0000740))\n9006:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D018069\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000609 \"A mechanoreceptor located in the acoustic maculae and the semicircular canals that mediates the sense of balance, movement, and head position. The vestibular hair cells are connected to accessory structures in such a way that movements of the head displace their stereocilia. This influences the membrane potential of the cells which relay information about movements via the vestibular part of the vestibulocochlear nerve to the brain stem.\")\n9172:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000626 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_1001502))\n9173:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000626 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_1001503))\n9174:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000626 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002428 obo:GO_0007608))\n9337:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.neurolex.org/wiki/Category:Bergmann_Glial_Cell\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000644 \"Type of radial astrocyte in the cerebellar cortex that have their cell bodies in the Purkinje cell layer and processes that extend into the molecular layer, terminating with bulbous endfeet at the pial surface. Bergmann glia express high densities of glutamate transporters that limit diffusion of the neurotransmitter glutamate during its release from synaptic terminals. Besides their role in early development of the cerebellum, Bergmann glia are also required for the pruning or addition of synapses.\")\n9429:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16687276\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinealocyte\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000652 \"This cell type produces and secretes melatonin and forms the pineal parenchyma. Extending from each cell body, which has a spherical, oval or lobulated mucleus, are one or more tortuous basophilic processes, containing parallel microtubules known as synaptic ribbons. These processes end in expanded terminal buds near capillaries or less, frequently, ependymal cells of the pineal recess. The terminal buds contain granular endoplasmic reticulum, mitochondria and electron-dense cored vesicles, which store monoamines and polypeptide hormones, release of which appears to require sympathetic innervation.\")\n9734:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"JB:jb\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000688 \"A fibroblast-like cell that provides support at neuromuscular junctions in vertebrates and are localized outside the synaptic basal lamina.\")\n9762:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18803315\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000692 \"perisynaptic Schwann cell\")\n10135:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dph\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000740 \"The set of neurons that receives neural inputs via bipolar, horizontal and amacrine cells. The axons of these cells make up the optic nerve.\")\n15030:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002098 \"A cardiac myocyte that is connected to other cardiac myocytes by transverse intercalated discs (GO:0014704) at a regular interval.\")\n17052:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15738192\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002285 \"A taste receptor cell that is characterized by morphologically identifiable synaptic contacts with the gustatory nerve fibers and expression of the synaptic membrane protein-25 (SNAP-25) and NCAM.\")\n17221:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19721455\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002300 \"A small medullary thymic epithelial cell with a spindle shape, often arranged in groups and connected to each other by large desmosomes and interdigitations. The cytoplasm is sparse, with scanty organelles and thick bundles of cytokeratin.\")\n21141:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11978858\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003011 \"A mono-stratified retinal ganglion cell that has a large dendritic field and a sparse dendritic arbor with post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S4.\")\n21154:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11978858\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003012 \"A mono-stratified retinal ganglion cell that has a large dendritic field and a medium dendritic arbor with post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S1 and S2.\")\n21168:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11978858\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003013 \"A mono-stratified retinal ganglion cell that has a large dendritic field, a medium dendritic arbor, and a long secondary dendrite shaft with post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S4.\")\n21195:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11978858\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003015 \"A G11 retinal ganglion cell that has post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S4 and is depolarized by illumination of its receptive field center.\")\n21207:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11978858\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003016 \"A G11 retinal ganglion cell that has post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S2 and is depolarized by decreased illumination of their receptive field center\")\n21219:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12209831\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003017 \"A retinal ganglion B cell that has post synaptic terminals in S2.\")\n21242:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12209831\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003020 \"A retinal ganglion cell C that has post-synaptic terminals in S2.\")\n21335:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003028 \"A monostratified retinal ganglion cell that has a small dendrite field with a dense dendrite arbor with post synaptic terminals in sublaminer layer S4.\")\n21348:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003029 \"A monostratified retinal ganglion cell that has a small soma, a small dendrite field with a dense dendrite arbor, and post synaptic terminals in sublaminer layer S2 and S3.\")\n21374:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003031 \"A monostratified retinal ganglion cell that has post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S4 and is depolarized by illumination of its receptive field center.\")\n21386:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003032 \"A monostratified retinal ganglion cell that has post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S2 and is depolarized by decreased illumination of their receptive field center\")\n21398:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003033 \"A monostratified retinal ganglion cell that has a large soma, a medium dendritic field with post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S3.\")\n21411:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003034 \"A monostratified retinal ganglion cell that has a small soma, an assymetric dendritic field with post synaptic terminals in sublaminar layer S3.\")\n21449:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003037 \"An M7 retinal ganglion cells with synaptic terminals in S4 and is depolarized by illumination of its receptive field center.\")\n21461:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003038 \"An M7 retinal ganglion cells with synaptic terminals in S2 and is depolarized by decreased illumination of their receptive field center\")\n21497:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003041 \"An M9 retinal ganglion cells with synaptic terminals in S2 and is depolarized by illumination of its receptive field center.\")\n21509:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16626866\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0003042 \"An M9 retinal ganglion cell with synaptic terminals in S4 that is depolarized by decreased illumination of their receptive field center\")\n21695:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12209831\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004119 \"A retinal ganglion cell B that has medium body size, medium dendritic field and dense dendritic arbor, and has post synaptic terminals in S2.\")\n21724:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12209831\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004121 \"A retinal ganglion cell B that has a very small but very dense dendritic field, and has post synaptic terminals in S2.\")\n21866:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004215 \"A type 5 cone bipolar cell with narrowly stratified post synaptic terminals.\")\n21908:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0004218 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:GO_0044316))\n21936:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24246263\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004221 \"A flag amacrine cell with post-synaptic terminals in S2 and S3. This cell type releases the neurotransmitter glycine.\")\n21949:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24246263\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004222 \"A flag amacrine cell with post-synaptic terminals in S3, S4, and S5. This cell type releases the neurotransmitter glycine.\")\n21963:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004223 \"A amacrine cell with a small dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals in S1, S2, S3, and S4. AB diffuse-1 amacrine cells have a tent-shaped dendritic arbor and undulate dendrites.\")\n21977:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004224 \"A broadly stratifying amacrine cell that has a small dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals in S2 and S3. This cell type releases the neurotransmitter glycine.\")\n21990:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004225 \"A broadly stratifying amacrine cell with a small dendritic field, straight dendrites and post-synaptic terminals in S1, S2, and S3.\")\n22003:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004226 \"An amacrine cell with a small dendritic field with post-synaptic terminals in S3 and S4.\")\n22015:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25630271\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004227 \"A bistratified amacrine cell with a small dendritic field. Flat bistratified amacrine cells have post-synaptic terminals both on the border of S1 and S2, and on the border of S3 and S4. This cell type releases the neurotransmitter glycine.\")\n22029:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004228 \"An amacrine cell with a small dendritic field that has post-synaptic terminals in S1, S2, S3, and S4.\")\n22056:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004230 \"A bistratified amacrine cell with a small dendritic field that has post-synaptic terminals in S1 and the border of S1-S2, and termination of a second arbor within the border of S2-S3 and S3.\")\n22071:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004231 \"A broadly stratifying amacrine cell with a small dendritic field and a complex dendritic arbor. Recurving diffuse amacrine cells have post-synaptic terminals in S2, S3, and S4.\")\n22084:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004232 \"An amacrine cell with a flat dendritic arbor and a medium dendritic field. Starburst amacrine cells have post-synaptic terminals in S2. This cell type releases the neurotransmitters gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) and acetylcholine.\")\n22097:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9194315\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004233 \"An amacrine cell with a medium dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals that stratify at S2, with a second stratification that occurs in S3 and S4. This cell type releases the neurotransmitter glycine.\")\n22111:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004234 \"An amacrine cell with a medium dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals in S2, and in S3-S4.\")\n22123:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004235 \"An amacrine cell with a medium dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals in S2 and S3. This cell type releases the neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA).\")\n22136:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004236 \"An amacrine cell with a medium dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals in S2, S3, and S4. This cell type releases the neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA).\")\n22150:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:10614594\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004237 \"A retinal amacrine cell with a medium dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals in S1, S2, S3, and S4. This cell type releases the neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA).\")\n22165:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004238 \"A bistratified amacrine cell with a medium dendritic field, a flat and sparse dendritic arbor, and post-synaptic terminals at the intersections of S1 and S2, and S3 and S4.\")\n22178:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004239 \"A bistratified amacrine cell with a medium dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals in S1-S2, and S4.\")\n22192:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004240 \"An amacrine cell with a wide dendritic field, dendrites in S1, and post-synaptic terminals in S1.\")\n22203:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004241 \"An amacrine cell with a wide dendritic field, dendrites in S2, and post-synaptic terminals in S1.\")\n22214:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004242 \"An amacrine cell with a wide dendritic field, dendrites in S3, and post-synaptic terminals in S3. Dendrites of this cell type are straight and minimally branched.\")\n22225:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004243 \"An amacrine cell with a wild dendritic field, dendrites in S3, and post-synaptic terminals in S3. Dendrites have spikes, cross over each other, and cover a larger volume than dendrties of W3-1 retinal amacrine cells.\")\n22236:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004244 \"An amacrine cell with a wide dendritic field, dendrites in S4, and post-synaptic terminals in S4.\")\n22247:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12486181\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9620701\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0004245 \"An amacrine cell with a wide dendritic field and post-synaptic terminals in S5. This cell type releases the neurotransmitters gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) and serotonin.\")\n22522:EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0005024 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000100 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008002)))\n22532:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0005025 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0011102))\n22714:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:1087183\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0007017 \"An epidermal cell with apical microvilli or a single apical projection have synaptic associations with nerve fibres in the epidermis.\")\n22843:EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0008010 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0015000 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008002)))\n22903:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0008019 \"A non-polarised cell precursor cell that is part of some mesenchyme, is associated with the cell matrix but is not connected to other cells and is capable of migration.\")\n23055:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0008037 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008047))\n23065:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0008038 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008046))\n23075:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0008039 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008002))\n23152:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0008048 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008039))\n24235:EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0009097 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0008002 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002491 obo:UBERON_0000068)))\n24243:EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0009098 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0008002 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002488 obo:UBERON_0000092) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002492 obo:UBERON_0007221)))\n24522:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31110133\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:5042759\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0010010 \"A GABAergic interneuron that is located in the molecular layer of the cerebellum. This cell receives excitatory inputs primarily from parallel fibers and plays a crucial role in feed-forward inhibition by suppressing the activity of Purkinje cells and modulating the output of the cerebellar cortex. The stellate cell is part of the local circuitry that contributes to the fine-tuning of motor coordination and a regulator of cerebellar blood flow via neurovascular coupling.\")\n24654:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:nv\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18677503\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2273099\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0011006 \"A cerebellar interneuron characterized by a spindle-shaped or triangular soma, parasagittally oriented and located at the border between the granular layer and the Purkinje cell layer. The Lugaro cell extends dendrites predominantly in the parasagittal plane, forming synaptic interactions with basket, stellate, and Golgi cells. Its axonal projections extend upward into the molecular layer, where they form a parasagittal plexus and emit long transverse collaterals that run parallel to the long axis of the cerebellar folia. The Lugaro cell is capable of co-releasing GABA and glycine, as evidenced by the expression of glutamate decarboxylase (GAD65/67) and the glycine transporter GlyT2.\")\n25401:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11596046\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28384468\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28949036\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35927027\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:7127069\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020003 \"A projection neuron that has its soma located in the internal segment of the globus pallidus (GPi) of primates, in the central “core” region. It expresses parvalbumin, is primarily GABAergic, and projects to motor thalamic nuclei including the ventral anterior, ventrolateral, and ventromedial nuclei. In the rodent homolog (entopeduncular nucleus), the core subregion is characterized by concentrated parvalbumin-positive neurons targeting the ventral anterior-ventral lateral thalamic nucleus and receives inputs from the dorsolateral striatum innervated by sensorimotor cortices (Miyamoto & Fukuda, 2022). This neuron corresponds to the sensorimotor output population of the GPi, as demonstrated in human tissue and cross-species comparative studies (Wallace et al., 2017) and by classic tracing in primates (Parent & De Bellefeuille, 1982; Parent et al., 2001).\")\n25460:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:10377365\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25876458\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29651049\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37905876\") terms:description obo:CL_0020008 \"Although they constitute only 1–3% of all striatal neurons, these interneurons form a major modulatory hub, integrating cortical, thalamic, and dopaminergic inputs and shaping striatal output dynamics (Gonzales & Smith, 2015; Wegman et al., 2024). Electrophysiologically, the neuron exhibits tonic autonomous firing at 3–10 Hz, broad action potentials, and a prominent hyperpolarization-activated inward current (Ih) (Bennett & Wilson, 1999; Goldberg & Reynolds, 2011). Functionally, it modulates corticostriatal and thalamostriatal synaptic transmission, dopamine release, and spiny projection neuron excitability through co-transmission of acetylcholine and GABA (Lozovaya et al., 2018; Wegman et al., 2024). This dual inhibitory and modulatory signaling underlies context-dependent control of synaptic plasticity, action selection, and reinforcement learning (Gonzales & Smith, 2015; Lozovaya et al., 2018).\")\n25632:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15153415\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1000222 \"A specialised neuroendocrine cell located in the gastric mucosa that regulates digestive processes including acid secretion and gut motility. This cell stores hormones in large dense core vesicles and synaptic-like microvesicles.\")\n28368:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MP:0009943\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1001435 \"The small neuron in the glomerular layer of the olfactory bulb whose dendrites arborize within a glomerulus, where it receives synaptic input from olfactory receptor cell axon terminals, and also engages in dendrodendritic interactions with mitral and tufted cell dendrites; uses both GABA and dopamine as a neurotransmitter.\")\n28425:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MP:0009955\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1001503 \"The principal glutaminergic neuron located in the outer third of the external plexiform layer of the olfactory bulb; a single short primary dendrite traverses the outer external plexiform layer and terminates within an olfactory glomerulus in a tuft of branches, where it receives the input from olfactory receptor neuron axon terminals; axons of the tufted cells transfer information to a number of areas in the brain, including the piriform cortex, entorhinal cortex, olfactory tubercle, and amygdala.\")\n29102:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32990595\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:8915580\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_2000027 \"A GABAergic inhibitory interneuron located in the molecular layer of the cerebellar cortex. It projects axons that form specialized synaptic structures around Purkinje cells, including pericellular baskets, which wrap around the Purkinje cell soma, and the pinceau: a brush-like terminal that contacts the initial segment of the Purkinje cell axon. The basket cell modulates Purkinje cell activity through both chemical (GABAergic) and non-synaptic (ephaptic) mechanisms. It is arranged in sagittal rows that align with Purkinje cell zones, contributing to the cerebellum's modular and topographically organized architecture. It plays an essential role in regulating motor coordination and potentially cognitive functions.\")\n29864:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4023000 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008046))\n29865:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4023000 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008047))\n30092:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4023020 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_4023005))\n30100:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4023021 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_4023006))\n30133:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27477017\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4023024 \"A lamp5 GABAergic cortical interneuron with layer-adapting morphology. NGC lamp5 cells have a small round soma, short dendrites, and a wide dense axonal arbor that tends to establish a dense axonal mesh with high connection probability both to themselves and L2 pyramidal cells. NGC lamp5 cells have unique synaptic properties that distinguish them from other GABAergic interneurons, including the release of GABA to the extracellular space via volume transmission, and the ability to produce GABA-B responses in connected postsynaptic targets.\")\n30279:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15378039\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27199673\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27477017\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37824655\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4023036 \"A transcriptomically distinct pvalb GABAergic interneuron located in the pallium. It is recognizable by the straight terminal axonal 'cartridges' of vertically oriented strings of synaptic boutons. Chandelier PV cells' boutons target exclusively the axon initial segment (AIS) of pyramidal cells, with a single cell innervating hundreds of pyramidal cells in a clustered manner.  These cells are fast-spiking.\")\n30467:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4023052 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0008039))\n30477:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4023053 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0005000))\n30995:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4023112 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002120 obo:CL_0000609))\n31008:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25355208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4023114 \"A vestibular afferent neuron which posseses a unique postsynaptic terminal, the calyx, which completely covers the basolateral walls of type I hair cells and receives input from multiple ribbon synapses.\")\n31227:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31881018\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"WikipediaVersioned:Bushy_cell&oldid=879061753\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4023163 \"A bushy cell that receives only few large excitatory endbulb synapses from auditory nerves. Spherical bush cells give excitatory input to the lateral and medial parts of the superior olive.\")\n31235:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31881018\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"WikipediaVersioned:Bushy_cell&oldid=879061753\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4023164 \"A bushy cell that receives a large number of medium-sized synapses, called modified endbulbs. Globular bushy cells extend to the superior olive on both sides of the brainstem where they give input to the bipolar neurons.\")\n32369:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22006647\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033027 \"An OFF diffuse bipolar cell that makes synaptic contact with both L/M and S-cone photoreceptors and only minimal contact with rod photoreceptors.\")\n32382:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25309346\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26500507\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27833534\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033028 \"An OFF diffuse bipolar cell that predominantly connects to ON parasol cells and lateral amacrine cells. This cell contains a large number of synaptic ribbons and a small axon arbor area.\")\n32413:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27833534\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32555229\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033031 \"An ON diffuse bipolar cell that predominantly connects to ON parasol cells and lateral amacrine cells.\")\n32422:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32555229\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033032 \"An ON diffuse bipolar cell that has a large dendritic field and large axon terminals, which show little or no overlap. This cell predominantly connects to narrow thorny ganglion cells.\")\n32440:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32555229\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033034 \"An ON bipolar cell with a small dendritic tree that forms most of the central (invaginating) elements opposite the synaptic ribbon at the cone triad.\")\n32648:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033053 \"A bistratfied retinal ganglion cell with a small dendritic field that has dendrites in the ON and OFF sublamina of the retinal inner plexiform layer and carries blue-ON/yellow-OFF signals. This cell receives bipolar and amacrine input to both the OFF and ON dendritic tree.\")\n33184:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19279265\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25904839\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34616062\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37139179\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37824655\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4042007 \"An astrocyte with highly branched protrusions, found in neocortex layers 2-6. It is involved with the formation and elimination of synapses, glutamate clearance, modulation of synaptic functions and regulation of blood flow in response to synaptic activity.\")\n33547:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30467465\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33742131\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4042037 \"Some striatal SST GABAergic neurons can co-release glutamate and generate excitation-inhibition responses in postsynaptic neurons. This interneuron type has the least dense dendritic branches in the striatum. In the dorsomedial striatum these neurons regulate the activity of striatal projection neurons.\")\n33742:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"DOI:10.1038/s41575-020-0271-2\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"DOI:10.1038/s41575-021-00561-y\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32152479\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34170401\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047038 \"A multipolar neuron in the myenteric plexus of the gastrointestinal tract, characterized by a small to medium-sized cell body and multiple short dendrites.  This neuron exhibits fast excitatory postsynaptic potentials and can be classified into stubby, spiny and hairy subtypes based on dendritic morphology. (Brehmer, 2021)\")\n34166:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33037211\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33311457\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33311464\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052025 \"A specialized muscle nucleus that is part of an extrafusal muscle fiber, clustered at the postsynaptic side of the neuromuscular junction. This nucleus exhibits a distinct transcriptional profile, including acetylcholine receptor (AChR) subunit genes. It regulates the production of proteins essential for maintaining the structure and function of the neuromuscular junction, supporting efficient nerve-muscle signaling for muscle contraction.\")\n34860:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31982322\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:39303139\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4072018 \"A neuron that generates rhythmic bursts of action potentials independently of synaptic input. This intrinsic property enables it to maintain oscillatory activity even when isolated from other neurons. It populates the brainstem, hypothalamus, basal ganglia, spinal cord, and cerebellum. It plays a crucial role in regulating circadian rhythms in the suprachiasmatic nucleus, generating respiratory rhythms in the preBötzinger complex, and synchronizing neural networks.\")\n34900:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072022 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0015004 obo:CLM_1000075))\n34918:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072027 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0015004 obo:CLM_1000082))\n34931:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072029 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0015004 obo:CLM_1000080))\n34946:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072031 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0015004 obo:CLM_1000083))\n34967:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072032 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0015004 obo:CLM_1000085))\n34982:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072034 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0015004 obo:CLM_1000074))\n34999:SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072035 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0015004 obo:CLM_1000081))\n35012:SubClass